# Production Secret File Permissions (INFRA-SEC-ENV-1)

Durable rules. Source of truth:
`docs/runbooks/production-secret-file-permissions-runbook.md` and
`docs/sprints/infra-sec-env-1-production-secret-file-permission-hardening.md`.

## Approved state

1. The production environment file is secret-bearing and **must never be
   world-readable**. `0644`, `0664`, `0666` are forbidden, permanently.
2. Other/world **read and write bits must always be zero**; group write must be
   zero.
3. Approved mode is derived from the actual runtime ownership requirement, never
   assumed.
4. Prefer `0600` when a single trusted owner is genuinely sufficient.
5. Use `0640` only when a real runtime group requires read. **On this host it
   does:** the deploy rebuilds the config cache as the PHP-FPM runtime user, and
   phpdotenv's `safeLoad()` swallows a permission error — so `0600 root:root`
   would silently produce a config cache with an empty application key and empty
   database credentials. Approved state: owner `root`, group = PHP-FPM runtime
   group, mode `0640`.
6. Secret-bearing backups (`.env.bak*`, `.env.backup*`, `.env.save*`, …) are
   `0600`. `.env.example` is public and untouched.
6b. **Database dumps are secret-bearing.** `storage/app/backups/**` (`*.sql`,
   `*.sql.gz`, `*.dump`, `*.tar.gz`) is full `pg_dump` output including national
   identity numbers and must never be world-readable — `0640` or tighter. Harden
   them by stripping the *other* bits ONLY; never chown them, or root and the
   runtime user lose access they legitimately have. `pg_dump > file` inherits the
   deploy user's `umask 022`, so the deploy and rollback scripts must `chmod`
   the dump immediately after creating it, not only at the end of the run.

## Enforcement

7. Deployment must enforce ownership and mode **every run** — never rely on
   historical filesystem state.
8. Deployment must **fail closed** when secret permissions are unsafe
   (`NOT GO`, non-zero exit), and must harden **before** it sources the file or
   lets the runtime user rebuild the config cache.
9. `scripts/rollback-vps.sh` must re-assert the same invariant, so rolling back
   cannot restore an unsafe mode.
10. The call to `scripts/harden-secret-permissions.sh` is a **required marker** in
    `config/deployment_rollback.php` for both the deploy and rollback contracts.
    Do not remove it — `foundation:deployment-rollback-check` runs in CI *and* in
    every deploy and will fail.

## Handling

11. **Never** print secret file contents to a terminal, CI log, pull request,
    doc, screenshot, or AI session. Use `stat`/`namei`/`getfacl`/`ls -l`
    metadata, or a `test -r` permission probe — never `cat`/`head`/`tail`/`grep`.
12. Temporary or backup copies of a secret must be created securely from birth
    (`umask 077` / mode `0600`), never created world-readable and chmodded after.
13. **Never** use a recursive `chmod`/`chown` over the application tree to fix a
    secret-file issue — it breaks directories, public assets, and scripts.
14. ACLs must not grant unexpected read access; a named-user/named-group read ACL
    on a secret file is a violation.
15. A symlinked secret file fails closed — the link target's mode is what
    actually governs access.
16. Every environment variant except `.env.example` stays gitignored and
    untracked (`.env.*` + `!.env.example`).

## Runtime

17. Least privilege must not break runtime. A permission change is **not** GO
    unless PHP-FPM, the queue worker, `config:cache`, Artisan bootstrap, and the
    health endpoints all still pass.
18. Do not grant broader group membership, and do not add unrelated accounts to
    the runtime group, merely to make a secret readable.
19. Do not disable the config cache as a workaround.

## Known residual

20. The co-tenant application on this host runs under the **same** `www-data`
    account, so a file mode cannot close same-UID read. This is a known,
    documented residual — do **not** claim co-tenant isolation as delivered.
    Closing it requires a dedicated runtime account (own FPM pool user + queue
    worker `User=` + `storage`/`bootstrap/cache` ownership) and is a separate
    sprint. `deploy-vps.sh` resolves `RUNTIME_USER` by grepping all FPM pools and
    taking `head -1`; make that pool-specific before any such migration.

## Process

21. `scripts/deploy-vps-runner.sh` runs **on the VPS**, never locally. Deploy
    start is not completion — `exit=0` plus `DEPLOY OK` are mandatory.
22. Future changes touching ownership, `chmod`, or `umask` in deploy automation
    must preserve these invariants.
23. INFRA-SEC-ENV-1 authorises no Legacy migration and no clinical data change;
    historical GO authorities remain immutable.
